Overlay patch 0013 — safe-area insets for the engine-drawn touch pad (iOS).

CLASS: (a) program-baseline parity. (Also a candidate upstream contribution:
nothing in it is PaperBoat-ios-specific except the name of the shell symbol.)

THE PROBLEM
-----------
Upstream draws its touch pad in the engine (`src/port/ui/TouchControls.cpp`),
laid out against ImGui's `DisplaySize`, and anchors its elements hard in the
corners: A at `(w - 2.6u, h - 3.0u)`, the menu button at `(2.0u, 1.8u)`, the
L/R/Z bars at `1.6u`/`3.4u` from the top. On the simulator that is fine,
because a simulator screen is a rectangle. On a real iPhone it is not: in
landscape the sensor housing eats ~59 pt off the leading edge and the home
indicator ~21 pt off the bottom, so the menu button or L sits *under the
housing* and Start sits *under the home indicator* — untappable, and on the
bottom edge competing with the system's own swipe-up gesture.

The engine cannot see any of this. `UIWindow.safeAreaInsets` is UIKit's, and
this LUS fork has no route from UIKit to the game. So the shell publishes the
insets and this patch spends them.

THE SHELL SIDE (no patch — it is our own file)
----------------------------------------------
`app/ios/PaperBoatIosShell.m` exports

    void PBIos_GetSafeAreaInsets(float* top, float* left, float* bottom, float* right);

the same linkage pattern overlay 0008 uses for `PBIos_IsBackgrounded` and 0012
for `PBIos_ThermalState`: a C symbol with a stable name, declared at file scope
on the engine side. It recomputes from the scene's key window whenever it is
called on the main thread (which is every frame — the game loop IS the main
thread on iOS), so rotation and scene reconnects need no notification plumbing,
and it logs every change with the window, its frame, the scale and the pixels
handed over (spec rule 6: a UIKit placement claim needs the view logging its
own frame).

**The insets arrive in DRAWABLE PIXELS, not points.** This fork runs ImGui and
all window geometry in drawable pixels on iOS (`Fast3dGui.cpp` sets
`io.DisplaySize` from `SDL_GetRendererOutputSize` with
`DisplayFramebufferScale = (1,1)` — docs/lus-divergence.md), and
`ComputeLayout` lays out against exactly that space. Points would be wrong by
the screen scale — 3x on every current iPhone — which would look like the fix
doing almost nothing rather than like a unit bug.

WHAT THE PATCH DOES — THREE SMALL THINGS
----------------------------------------
1. `SafeRect()` — the one place that reads the shell. Off iOS it reports the
   full display, so **zero-inset behaviour is byte-identical to today** and the
   macOS oracle compiles and behaves unchanged.

2. `SafeDefault()` inside `LoadPos`, applied to the FALLBACK ONLY. Every
   element's default — the 15 buttons, the stick rest, the menu button — is
   already routed through `LoadPos`, so one line covers the whole pad. A saved
   custom position (`gTouchControls.Layout.<id>.{X,Y}`) takes the early return
   above and is never touched: **the user's layout always wins**, which is also
   what stops this patch from silently rewriting layouts people already made.

   The transform is a linear remap of the full display rect onto the safe rect
   (`x' = left + x * safeW / w`), not a per-edge "if it is near an edge, push
   it in". Both put every default inside the safe rect; the remap does it
   without a near-an-edge threshold to tune, keeps the relative arrangement of
   the pad exactly as upstream drew it, and does something sensible for the
   centre-anchored elements (Start at `w * 0.5`) instead of having to pick a
   side for them. The cost is that gaps shrink by the inset fraction (~3 % on
   an iPhone Air) — invisible, and in the right direction.

3. The layout editor clamps drags to the safe rect instead of to `(0..w,
   0..h)`, so a user cannot park a button under the housing by hand either.
   Not cosmetic: `SavePos` normalizes against the FULL display, so an
   unclamped drag writes a position that no later inset handling can rescue.

WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
--------------------------------
* Element SIZES are untouched. Positions are the safety problem; sizes are a
  taste problem, and overlay 0014 is where the default arrangement is argued.
* The edit-mode Done/Reset pills and the hint text are top-centre, where the
  landscape inset is zero on every iPhone; they stay where upstream put them
  rather than being dragged into a patch hunk that would collide with 0014's.
  (`apply-overlay.sh` probes "already applied" by reverse-applying at fuzz=0,
  so two patches sharing context lines break the SECOND build — spec trap.)

